# Quad X baseline for the custom ArduPilot JSON SITL model.
#
# Everything not set here comes from copter.parm, which targets a vehicle of
# one to two kilograms. This airframe is 0.240 kg with a roll/pitch inertia of
# 0.00022 kg m^2 and a yaw inertia of 0.00038, so it reacts far faster. Attitude
# gains are deliberately still at their defaults; see workflow/backlog.md.
FRAME_CLASS 1
FRAME_TYPE 1

# The SDF maps 1100-1900 us onto zero-to-full rotor speed, but ArduPilot's
# output range defaults to 1000-2000. Below 1100 the plugin computed a negative
# rotor speed, so a disarmed vehicle sat on the floor turning itself slowly and
# failed the compass field check ("Check mag field xy diff"). Matching the two
# ranges also stops throttle above 1900 from exceeding the modelled maximum.
MOT_PWM_MIN 1100
MOT_PWM_MAX 1900

# The simulated rotor produces thrust proportional to the square of commanded
# speed, and speed is linear in throttle, so full expo inverts that curve exactly.
MOT_THST_EXPO 1.0

# Hover throttle follows from the calibrated rotors rather than being guessed.
# Thrust to weight is 5.9, so hover needs a thrust fraction of 1/5.9 = 0.169,
# and MOT_THST_EXPO 1.0 makes the actuator output sqrt(0.169) = 0.41. Written
# down explicitly so the first flight of a test is deterministic instead of
# waiting for MOT_HOVER_LEARN.
MOT_THST_HOVER 0.41

# Attitude rate gains scaled for this airframe's control authority, which is
# what the stock gains get wrong. A full roll demand moves two rotors up and two
# down by half of max thrust each, so the peak roll moment is
# 2 * 3.485 N * 0.04455 m = 0.311 N m against a roll inertia of 0.00022 kg m^2:
# 1411 rad/s^2, or 80900 deg/s^2. A 1.5 kg ten-inch quad at a thrust-to-weight
# ratio of 2 manages about 10500 deg/s^2, so this airframe has 7.7 times the
# angular acceleration per unit of controller output and the defaults are 7.7
# times too high.
#
# Left at stock, the rate loop saturated: one rotor pinned at the MOT_SPIN_MIN
# floor of 597 rad/s while the others ran 1500 to 2200, the saturating set
# rotating between rotors. Copter then gave throttle away to keep attitude
# authority, so the log showed ThO 0.000 and a commanded descent of -2.5 m/s
# while the vehicle flung itself to 54 m. Defaults divided by 7.7; ArduPilot's
# own tuning guide also says smaller propellers need lower rate gains.
ATC_RAT_RLL_P 0.018
ATC_RAT_RLL_I 0.018
ATC_RAT_RLL_D 0.0005
ATC_RAT_PIT_P 0.018
ATC_RAT_PIT_I 0.018
ATC_RAT_PIT_D 0.0005
ATC_RAT_YAW_P 0.023
ATC_RAT_YAW_I 0.0023
